\title[Shape Transfers, Level Tracks the Population]{Shape Transfers, Level Tracks the Population:\\ Exact Arithmetic and Regression Laws for a Per-$N$ Quadratic-Sieve Yield Dial}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The quadratic sieve factors an integer $N$ by sieving the values of $Q(x)=x^2-N$ over a factor base of small primes, and its running time is dominated by the \emph{yield}, the fraction of sieved candidates that are smooth. Integers of identical size can have markedly different yields. A cross-scale experimental study adopted the per-$N$ predictor $\mathrm{rate}(N)\approx -0.0035+0.01156\cdot\QR(N)$, where $\QR(N)$ counts the odd primes $p\le 100$ passing Euler's test for $N$, and summarised its behaviour by the slogan ``the shape transfers perfectly, the level tracks each population.'' We show that each half of the slogan has an exact counterpart. (i) \emph{Shape:} for each odd prime $p$ the number of roots of $x^2\equiv N\pmod p$ is $1+\Leg{N}{p}$, Euler's test is exactly $\Leg{N}{p}=1$, and the total root mass over any set $S$ of odd primes equals $2\QR_S(N)+\#\{p\in S:p\mid N\}$. (ii) \emph{Level:} in every complete residue system modulo $p$ exactly $(p-1)/2$ classes pass, so the population mean of $\QR_S$ over a common period is $\sum_{p\in S}\frac{(p-1)/2}{p}\in[|S|/3,|S|/2)$; for the 24 odd primes up to $100$ the mean feature lies in $[8,12)$ and the mean adopted rate in $[0.08898,0.13522)$. (iii) \emph{Transfer:} for any finite sample, the in-sample $R^2$ of a transferred slope $b$ with refitted level is exactly $\rho^2-(b-\hat\beta)^2S_{xx}/S_{yy}$. (iv) \emph{Floor:} for any feature-only predictor, the residual sum of squares is pure error plus lack of fit. We also show that the reported pair (transfer $R^2=0.2719$, $\rho^2=0.2717$) cannot consist of two in-sample statistics of one sample, and that the adopted dial is negative on the semiprime $163520117=2027\cdot 80671$, so it must be clipped.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The quadratic sieve and per-$N$ yield}
Let $N$ be an odd composite integer with no small prime factors. The quadratic sieve searches for integers $x$ near $\sqrt N$ such that $Q(x)=x^2-N$ factors completely over a factor base of small primes. Relations $x^2\equiv Q(x)\pmod N$ with smooth $Q(x)$ are combined by linear algebra over $\mathbb{F}_2$ into a congruence of squares $a^2\equiv b^2\pmod N$, and $\gcd(a-b,N)$ yields a factor with probability at least $1/2$ per independent congruence.

The dominant cost is sieving. For each factor-base prime $p$ one locates the residues $r\bmod p$ with $p\mid Q(r)$ and adds $\log p$ at every position $x\equiv r\pmod p$. Only primes $p$ for which $N$ is a quadratic residue contribute roots; the classical Knuth--Schroeppel multiplier heuristic exploits this. The same principle applies to a fixed $N$: two moduli of equal size can differ substantially in how many small primes are ``friendly,'' and hence in yield.

\subsection{The experimental dial}
A three-scale study measured per-$N$ smoothness yields at smoothness parameters $u=2.5$ and $u=3.5$ and regressed them on
\[
\QR(N)=\#\{p \text{ odd prime},\ p\le 100:\ p\nmid N,\ N^{(p-1)/2}\equiv 1 \pmod p\}.
\]
Its reported findings, used here as empirical backdrop, were: a base effect at all three scales with correlation $r\in[0.497,0.521]$ at $u=2.5$; confirmation of the primary hypothesis on a test split ($R^2=0.3041$, slope ratio $1.128$); near-perfect cross-scale transfer (transferred slope with refitted level gave $R^2=0.2719$ against a target squared correlation $0.2717$), with slopes in band in $4$ of $4$ cells; a null result ($+0.009$ in $R^2$) for a $1/p$-weighted feature; and residual-to-floor ratios of $1.31$ at $u=2.5$ and $1.05$ at $u=3.5$. The adopted form was $\mathrm{rate}(N)\approx-0.0035+0.01156\cdot\QR(N)$.

\subsection{Contributions}
We prove: the Root-Count Law and Shape Theorem (Section~\ref{sec:shape}); the Half-Pass Law and Population Mean Theorem (Section~\ref{sec:level}); the range of the dial and a negative witness (Section~\ref{sec:dial}); the Transfer Law with its corollaries and an impossibility result (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}); and the Floor Decomposition (Section~\ref{sec:floor}). Section~\ref{sec:interp} interprets the experiment; Sections~\ref{sec:alg}--\ref{sec:future} give algorithms, applications, limitations and future work.

\section{Setting and definitions}
Throughout, $p$ is an odd prime and $N$ a nonnegative integer.

\begin{definition}
The Legendre symbol $\Leg{N}{p}$ is $0$ if $p\mid N$, $+1$ if $N$ is a nonzero square modulo $p$, and $-1$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{root count} is $\roots_p(N)=\#\{r\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}: p\mid r^2-N\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$N$ \emph{passes the Euler test at $p$}, written $\pass_p(N)$, if $p\nmid N$ and $N^{(p-1)/2}\equiv1\pmod p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a finite set $S$ of odd primes, $\QR_S(N)=\#\{p\in S:\pass_p(N)\}$. Let $P_{100}=\{3,5,\dots,97\}$ be the odd primes at most $100$; $|P_{100}|=24$ and $\QR=\QR_{P_{100}}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The adopted dial is $\dial(q)=-\frac{7}{2000}+\frac{289}{25000}\,q$, i.e.\ $-0.0035+0.01156\,q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a finite nonempty sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i\in s}$ of size $n$ with means $\bar x,\bar y$, put
$S_{xx}=\sum(x_i-\bar x)^2$, $S_{yy}=\sum(y_i-\bar y)^2$, $S_{xy}=\sum(x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y)$,
$\SSE(a,b)=\sum(y_i-a-bx_i)^2$, $R^2(a,b)=1-\SSE(a,b)/S_{yy}$, $\hat\beta=S_{xy}/S_{xx}$ and $\rho^2=S_{xy}^2/(S_{xx}S_{yy})$. The \emph{transfer $R^2$} of a slope $b$ is $R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}(b)=R^2(\bar y-b\bar x,\,b)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Shape: the feature is exact root mass}\label{sec:shape}

\begin{theorem}[Root-Count Law]
For every odd prime $p$ and every $N$, $\roots_p(N)=1+\Leg{N}{p}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reduction modulo $p$ identifies $\roots_p(N)$ with the number of square roots of $\bar N$ in $\mathbb{F}_p$. If $\bar N=0$ there is one root; if $\bar N=c^2\ne0$ the roots are $\pm c$, distinct as $p$ is odd; otherwise there are none.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
For every odd prime $p$, $\pass_p(N)\iff\Leg{N}{p}=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Euler's criterion $N^{(p-1)/2}\equiv\Leg{N}{p}\pmod p$. Since $p>2$, $-1\not\equiv1\pmod p$, so for $p\nmid N$ the residue $1$ occurs exactly when the symbol is $+1$; if $p\mid N$ both sides fail.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Per-prime split]
$\roots_p(N)=2\,[\pass_p(N)]+[\,p\mid N\,]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Shape Theorem]
For every finite set $S$ of odd primes and every $N$,
\[
\sum_{p\in S}\roots_p(N)=2\,\QR_S(N)+\#\{p\in S:p\mid N\}.
\]
In particular, if no prime of $S$ divides $N$, the total root mass equals $2\,\QR_S(N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Sum the per-prime split over $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Periodicity]
$p\mid(x+p)^2-N\iff p\mid x^2-N$, since $(x+p)^2-N=(x^2-N)+p(2x+p)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}
If $P(x+p)\iff P(x)$ for all $x$, then for every $L$ the number of $x\in[0,Lp)$ with $P(x)$ is $L$ times the number of $x\in[0,p)$ with $P(x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sieve hits on an interval]
The number of $x\in[0,Lp)$ with $p\mid x^2-N$ equals $L\cdot\roots_p(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Weighted sieve yield]
If $M$ is a common multiple of the primes in $S$, then
\[
\sum_{p\in S}\#\{x\in[0,M):p\mid x^2-N\}=\sum_{p\in S}\frac{M}{p}\bigl(2[\pass_p(N)]+[\,p\mid N\,]\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\section{Level: the population decides the mean}\label{sec:level}

\begin{lemma}
$\sum_{a=0}^{p-1}\roots_p(a)=p$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exchange sums: each $r\in[0,p)$ is a root for exactly one $a\in[0,p)$, namely $a\equiv r^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Half-Pass Law]
For every odd prime $p$ exactly $(p-1)/2$ residues $a\in[0,p)$ pass the Euler test.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Summing the per-prime split over $a\in[0,p)$ gives $p=2\cdot\#\{\text{passing }a\}+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Population Sum and Mean]
Let $M>0$ be a common multiple of the primes in $S$. Then
\[
\sum_{N=0}^{M-1}\QR_S(N)=\sum_{p\in S}\frac{M}{p}\cdot\frac{p-1}{2},
\qquad
\frac1M\sum_{N=0}^{M-1}\QR_S(N)=\mu_S:=\sum_{p\in S}\frac{(p-1)/2}{p}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exchange sums; the pass predicate is $p$-periodic in $N$, so each prime contributes $M/p$ periods of $(p-1)/2$ passes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
For every odd prime $p$, $\frac13\le\frac{(p-1)/2}{p}<\frac12$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $p=2k+1$, $k\ge1$: $3k\ge2k+1$ and $2k<2k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mean bounds]
If $S\neq\emptyset$ then $|S|/3\le\mu_S<|S|/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Population level of the adopted dial]
For every common multiple $M>0$ of the primes in $P_{100}$, the population mean of $\QR(N)$ over $0\le N<M$ lies in $[8,12)$ and the population mean of $\dial(\QR(N))$ lies in $[\frac{4449}{50000},\frac{6761}{50000})=[0.08898,0.13522)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use $|P_{100}|=24$, the mean bounds, and the fact that an affine dial commutes with averaging.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Numerically $\mu_{P_{100}}=12-\frac12\sum_{p\in P_{100}}1/p\approx11.3486$ and the population-mean dial is approximately $0.1277$. The mean is a population constant; the deviation $\QR(N)-\mu$ is the per-$N$ signal.
\end{remark}

\section{Range of the dial and a negative witness}\label{sec:dial}

\begin{proposition}
For integers $q\ge0$, $\dial(q)>0\iff q\ge1$; and $\dial(\QR(N))\le\frac{13697}{50000}=0.27394$ for every $N$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\dial(0)=-\frac{7}{2000}$, $\dial(1)=\frac{403}{50000}$, the dial is increasing, and $\QR(N)\le24$ with $\dial(24)=\frac{13697}{50000}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Negative witness]
Let $N_0=163520117$. Then $N_0=2027\cdot80671$ with both factors prime, $\QR(N_0)=0$, $\sum_{p\in P_{100}}\roots_p(N_0)=0$, and $\dial(\QR(N_0))=-0.0035<0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A finite computation shows that $N_0$ is a non-residue modulo each of the 24 odd primes up to $100$ and divisible by none; the root-mass claim follows from the Shape Theorem.
\end{proof}

Hence the dial must be used in clipped form $\max(0,\dial(\QR(N)))$.

\section{The Transfer Law}\label{sec:transfer}
Fix a finite nonempty sample with $S_{xx}>0$ and $S_{yy}>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Level/shape decomposition]
$\SSE(a,b)=n(\bar y-a-b\bar x)^2+(S_{yy}-2bS_{xy}+b^2S_{xx})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split $y_i-a-bx_i=(\bar y-a-b\bar x)+((y_i-\bar y)-b(x_i-\bar x))$ and use that centred deviations sum to zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Level refit is optimal]
$\SSE(\bar y-b\bar x,b)\le\SSE(a,b)$ for all $a,b$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}
$\SSE(\bar y-b\bar x,b)=S_{xx}(b-\hat\beta)^2+\bigl(S_{yy}-S_{xy}^2/S_{xx}\bigr)$; consequently $S_{xy}^2\le S_{xx}S_{yy}$ and $\rho^2\le1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Transfer Law]
For every slope $b$,
\[
R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}(b)=\rho^2-(b-\hat\beta)^2\,\frac{S_{xx}}{S_{yy}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:transfer}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}(\hat\beta)=\rho^2$.
\item $R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}(b)\le\rho^2$ for every $b$, with equality iff $b=\hat\beta$.
\item $R^2(a,b)\le\rho^2$ for every affine predictor.
\item For every $\varepsilon$: $\rho^2-R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}(b)\le\varepsilon\iff(b-\hat\beta)^2\le\varepsilon\,S_{yy}/S_{xx}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Impossibility of the reported pair]
No sample with $S_{xx},S_{yy}>0$ and no affine predictor satisfy both $|R^2(a,b)-0.2719|\le0.00005$ and $|\rho^2-0.2717|\le0.00005$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
These force $R^2(a,b)\ge0.27185>0.27175\ge\rho^2$, contradicting Corollary~\ref{cor:transfer}(3).
\end{proof}

The two published numbers must therefore come from different samples; the honest reading is that the transfer gap is indistinguishable from zero, i.e.\ the transferred slope lies in a narrow band around the target slope.

\section{The pure-error floor}\label{sec:floor}

\begin{definition}
For a sample with feature values $x_i$, the group mean $m(i)$ is the mean of $y_j$ over $\{j:x_j=x_i\}$, and the pure error is $\PE=\sum_i(y_i-m(i))^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
For a nonempty group and constant $c$: $\sum(y_i-c)^2=\sum(y_i-\bar y)^2+n(\bar y-c)^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Floor Decomposition]
For every $g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$,
\[
\sum_i(y_i-g(x_i))^2=\PE+\sum_i(m(i)-g(x_i))^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition the sample by feature value and apply the lemma on each fibre, where $g$ is constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\PE$ is a lower bound for every feature-only predictor, attained exactly by predictors agreeing with the group means on the sample; in particular $\PE\le\SSE(a,b)$ for every affine dial, and $\PE\le S_{yy}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Interpreting the experiment}\label{sec:interp}
\emph{Shape.} For moduli free of small factors the feature is exactly half the root mass of the 24 smallest odd sieve primes, an identity independent of the size of $N$; this explains in-band slope transfer, while $\log p$ weights and larger primes explain why the correlation is about $0.5$ rather than $1$.
\emph{Level.} The mean feature is a population constant and mean yield depends on the regime, so the intercept must be refitted; refitting never hurts.
\emph{Weighted null.} The weighted and unweighted features are linear in the same pass indicators with weights varying by a factor of about $33$, hence nearly collinear.
\emph{Floor.} Ratios $1.31$ and $1.05$ are necessarily at least $1$; at $u=3.5$ the dial is within $5\%$ of the best function of $\QR$, while at $u=2.5$ the excess is lack of fit to the group means.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:alg}
\begin{algorithm}[Per-$N$ yield dial]
Set $q\leftarrow0$; for each $p\in P_{100}$, if $N\bmod p\ne0$ and $N^{(p-1)/2}\bmod p=1$, increment $q$; return $\max(0,-0.0035+0.01156\,q)$. Cost: $24$ reductions of $N$ and $24$ small modular exponentiations.
\end{algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[Transfer with level refit]
Fit the slope $b$ on the source; on the target compute $\bar x,\bar y,S_{xx},S_{yy},S_{xy},\hat\beta,\rho^2$; set $a=\bar y-b\bar x$; report $R^2_{\mathrm{tr}}=\rho^2-(b-\hat\beta)^2S_{xx}/S_{yy}$ and declare the slope in band iff $(b-\hat\beta)^2\le\varepsilon S_{yy}/S_{xx}$, computing all statistics on one sample. Cost $O(n)$.
\end{algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[Floor attribution]
Group by feature value, compute $\PE$, and report $\SSE(a,b)/\PE\ge1$ together with the lack of fit $\sum_i(m(i)-a-bx_i)^2$. Cost $O(n)$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications}
The clipped dial gives a cheap pre-sieve yield estimate for parameter calibration (interval length, factor-base size, time budget), with level refitted per regime and slope carried over. The Shape Theorem quantifies multiplier selection: replacing $N$ by $kN$ changes root mass by exactly twice the change in $\QR$. The Transfer Law and impossibility theorem are exact consistency checks for any regression-transfer study, and the Floor Decomposition applies to any discrete predictor.

\section{Discussion and limitations}
The exact results concern the mechanism (root mass), the population mean of the feature, and regression algebra; the correlation with yield and the numerical slope are empirical. The population theorems describe a uniform population over a complete common period, which real samples of fixed-size semiprimes only approximate. The dial predicts constant-factor variations in yield and does not affect the asymptotic complexity of factoring algorithms.

\section{Future work}\label{sec:future}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \emph{Log-weighted root-mass equivalence:} compute, under an independent fair-coin model for Legendre bits, the correlation between the $1/p$-weighted root mass and the unweighted count over $p\le100$; the conjecture is that it is at least $0.95$.
\item \emph{Slope-band certificate:} recompute both statistics on one held-out sample per cell and verify that the gap equals $(b_{\mathrm{src}}-\hat\beta_{\mathrm{tgt}})^2S_{xx}/S_{yy}$, with in-band cells satisfying $(b-\hat\beta)^2\le10^{-3}S_{yy}/S_{xx}$.
\item \emph{Clipped and nonlinear dials:} quantify how much of the $31\%$ excess at $u=2.5$ the group-mean profile recovers.
\item \emph{Beyond $p\le100$:} extend to larger primes with $\log p$ weights, where the weighted-yield theorem remains exact over a common period.
\end{enumerate}

